[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one parser bug, one fix queued. Its reports say
otherwise. Read by tenant, channel and body, they fall into separate problems, and the queued
fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket. Shipping the parser fix alone leaves the
others open.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/anthnyb/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/anthnyb/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2',
    shift: 'https://github.com/anthnyb/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3',
  }}
/>

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. A day-first tenant's
  7 April becomes 4 July. Every channel, every tenant. This is the fix that is queued.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants'
  people type month first, as told, and the form reads the date day first. Only the `form`
  channel on booking-v3 tenants. The parser fix does not touch it; the hint and the form's
  reading have to agree with the tenant's locale.
- **`shift`**: the date moves one day earlier, not a swap: "Tuesday 12 May" arrives as
  "Monday 11 May", a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday and a client in Toronto gets Monday. It
  depends on the recipient's time zone, not the tenant's date order. The desk noted these
  reports as one-day moves. Its #4471 table files them under `parser` by tenant, so they are
  counted there too and footnoted.

## How the split was made

- One id, one report: the export repeats some reports across pages, so they are counted once.
- `parser` and `hint` come from `causeOf` in `#lib/kb/causes`: tenant locale, booking form and
  channel. Intake's tag is not used: it guesses from the reporter's browser, and it disagrees
  with the tenant often.
- `shift` comes from the desk's own reading (`oneDayMove` in desk notes). Their dates do not
  swap, so they get their own ticket; the counts stay the desk's, as in the #4471 table.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. Every one of
these problems produces the same subjects: "wrong day", "date is wrong". Matching on subject
could not tell them apart.

